 ## Background
-GaussianDiffusion was proposed in [251] (cited in [S1][S2, unofficial]) as a method to incorporate structured noises from multiple viewpoints to alleviate the Janus problem and use variational 3DGS for better generation results by mitigating floaters. It is part of a broader trend of using generative models (diffusion or feed-forward) to predict 3D Gaussian parameters directly, bridging the gap between continuous latent spaces and discrete 3D Gaussian sets [S1]. The method relies on the GaussianCube representation, which converts a constant number of Gaussians into a voxelized format via optimal transport [S1].
+GaussianDiffusion was proposed in the context of recent advances in 3D Gaussian splatting [S1][S2, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in single-view 3D generation, such as the Janus problem and floaters, by integrating diffusion models with a GaussianCube representation [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method builds on the principles of hybrid and diffusion representations, where Gaussian attributes are predicted directly from conditioning signals [Parent]. It is related to other methods like LucidDreamer, which uses interval score matching, and GVGEN, which uses a 3D Gaussian volume representation [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
